## Summary

Three code paths acquire a daemon connection via pool.Get but use unconditional `defer pool.Put(client)` without discarding on RPC error. When an RPC call fails (timeout, protocol error, etc.), the connection has leftover bytes in its read buffer. Returning it to the pool via Put causes the next borrower to read stale bytes, producing JSON unmarshal corruption errors. This task adds the `rpcOK bool` + conditional Discard pattern to these three code paths, matching the pattern already used by all other pool consumers (e.g., `handlers/misc/config.go:getWorkspacePath`, `handlers/health/health.go`, `handlers/git/graph.go`).

## Technical Approach

Apply the same `rpcOK` guard pattern already established across 9+ files in the codebase:

```go
rpcOK := false
defer func() {
    if rpcOK {
        pool.Put(client)
    } else {
        pool.Discard(client)
    }
}()
// ... RPC call ...
rpcOK = true
```

For the terminal package, the `ConfigConnectionGetter` interface currently lacks `Discard`, so it must be added. The `TermConfigPoolAdapter` (which implements this interface) must also gain a `Discard` method. The `handlers/misc/config.go` package already solved this identically for its own `configConnectionGetter` interface — we follow the exact same pattern.

**No new abstractions.** This is a mechanical fix applying an established pattern to 3 missed code paths.

## Conventions Established

None new — this follows the existing `rpcOK` + conditional Discard convention established by the v2 port (commit bc1a0f0a) and already present in 9+ files. This task simply brings the 3 remaining code paths into compliance.

