## Summary

Create the OpenInEditor dropdown component that lets users open a file/workspace path in any detected external editor or tool. The component consumes the useEditors() hook (from loomcli-963rg.5) to display available editors and launches the selected one via the openEditor() API. It follows the PriorityDropdown custom dropdown pattern (not native select) for full control over keyboard navigation, styling, and the "Launching..." transient state.

## Technical Approach

**Pattern:** Follow the PriorityDropdown pattern from IssueDetailPanel — custom button trigger with absolute-positioned menu, click-outside detection, keyboard navigation (Arrow keys, Enter, Escape), and ref-based DOM management. This is preferred over native `<select>` because:
- Need custom "Launching..." transient state on the selected option
- Want to show editor icon indicators per option
- Need consistent cross-browser styling

**Props:** `{ path: string, className?: string }` as specified. The component is self-contained — it calls useEditors() internally and handles the openEditor() call directly. No callback props needed since the action (launching an editor) is fire-and-forget with visual feedback.

**Launching state:** When the user selects an editor, call openEditor(editorId, path). During the async call, show "Launching..." text on the trigger button with a brief animation. On success, revert to "Open in..." after ~1.5s. On error, show error text briefly then revert.

**Empty/loading states:**
- While useEditors() is loading: button shows "Open in..." but is disabled
- If no detected editors: dropdown menu shows a single disabled item "No editors detected"
- If useEditors() errors: button is disabled, tooltip or title attr explains

**CSS:** Use existing CSS variable tokens (--color-bg, --color-border, --color-text, --color-bg-hover, --radius-md, --space-2, --z-dropdown, --transition-fast, etc.) from variables.css. CSS modules for scoping.

