## Summary

Three listener arrays in `client.ts` (`authStateListeners`, `daemonUnavailableListeners`, `authTokenExpiredListeners`) use a push+active-flag pattern: unsubscribe sets `listener.active = false` but never removes the entry. In a long-lived SPA session, dead entries accumulate on every mount/unmount cycle. This task replaces all three with `Set<callback>` so unsubscribe actually removes the entry.

## Technical Approach

Replace the array-of-`{ callback, active }` pattern with a plain `Set<CallbackType>`. A `Set` is the right data structure because:
- `set.add(cb)` to subscribe, `set.delete(cb)` to unsubscribe — O(1) removal, no dead entries
- Iterating a Set is safe mid-iteration when using `for...of` (additions after current position are visited; deletions of unvisited items are skipped — but our notify functions don't add/remove during iteration, so this is moot)
- No ID generation needed (unlike Map), no index bookkeeping (unlike splice)
- The public API signatures (`onAuthStateChange`, `onDaemonUnavailable`, `onAuthTokenExpired`) remain identical: each returns `() => void`

**Trade-off considered**: The task description suggests `Map + splice`. A `Map<number, callback>` would require an incrementing ID counter and is more complex with no benefit over `Set` for this use case. `Set` is simpler, idiomatic, and achieves the goal.

**Duplicate listener edge case**: If the same function reference is registered twice, `Set` deduplicates. In practice, React `useEffect` creates new closures each render, so this won't occur. If a caller did pass the same reference twice, deduplication is the safer behavior (prevents double-firing).

