Add one new test to verify that unsubscribe actually removes the entry (the core bug assertion). This test needs access to the internal Set size, which is not exported. Two approaches:

**Preferred approach**: Test the observable behavior — register N listeners, unsubscribe all, then verify that a subsequent state change doesn't fire any callbacks. The existing "unsubscribe prevents further callbacks" tests already cover this for each listener type. The key new test is a **leak regression test**: subscribe and unsubscribe in a loop (e.g., 1000 times), then verify that the module doesn't hold references to any of the callbacks. Since we can't inspect Set size directly, we verify by registering one more listener, triggering a state change, and confirming only the final listener fires (not any stale ones). This proves no accumulation.

**Concrete new test** (add in the auth state tracking describe block):
```ts
it("unsubscribe removes listener — no dead-entry accumulation", () => {
  // Subscribe and unsubscribe 100 times to simulate mount/unmount cycles
  const staleCallbacks: ReturnType<typeof vi.fn>[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
    const cb = vi.fn();
    staleCallbacks.push(cb);
    const unsub = onAuthStateChange(cb);
    unsub();
  }

  // One live listener
  const liveCallback = vi.fn();
  const liveUnsub = onAuthStateChange(liveCallback);

  try {
    setAuthToken("leak-test");
    // Only the live listener should fire
    expect(liveCallback).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
    // None of the stale callbacks should fire
    for (const cb of staleCallbacks) {
      expect(cb).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
    }
  } finally {
    liveUnsub();
  }
});
```

