## Summary

Add a "System Health" icon button to `BOTTOM_ITEMS` in `NavRail.tsx` (above Settings), using a heartbeat/pulse SVG icon. Add a `degraded?: boolean` prop to `NavRailProps` — when true and the item is `daemon-health`, render the existing `unreadIndicator` dot with `data-severity="degraded"` so CSS can style it amber. Add the corresponding CSS rule in `NavRail.module.css` for the amber degraded badge.

This gives users a persistent, at-a-glance signal that daemon health is degraded, and provides the nav entry point to the daemon health dashboard (wired by sibling task `.9`).

## Technical Approach

**Existing pattern — BOTTOM_ITEMS**: The NavRail already has `TOP_ITEMS` and `BOTTOM_ITEMS` arrays. Settings is currently the only bottom item. Adding daemon-health as the first entry in `BOTTOM_ITEMS` (before Settings) places it above the gear icon, which is the natural position for a system status indicator — more prominent than settings, but still in the utility section.

**New `degraded` prop**: A simple `degraded?: boolean` prop on `NavRailProps`. This is more explicit than overloading the existing `badges` prop (which is `Partial<Record<ViewMode, boolean>>` — a generic unread indicator). The task description specifically calls for a `degraded` prop, and the amber badge has different semantics (system health warning vs unread content notification). Keeping it separate makes the API clear.

**Reuse `unreadIndicator` CSS class with `data-severity` attribute**: The rendering logic adds `data-severity="degraded"` to the indicator span when the degraded prop is true and the item is `daemon-health`. A new CSS rule `.unreadIndicator[data-severity="degraded"]` overrides the default blue background with `var(--color-warning)` (amber). This follows the established `data-severity` pattern from `ErrorIndicators.module.css`.

**Heartbeat SVG icon**: An inline SVG heartbeat/pulse icon matching the existing icon style (16x16 viewBox rendered at current size, `stroke="currentColor"`, `strokeWidth="2"`, `fill="none"`). The icon depicts a heart with a pulse line, following the visual language of the other NavRail icons (simple line-art, no fill, 2px stroke).

**Show degraded indicator logic**: The degraded dot shows when ALL of:
1. `degraded` prop is `true`
2. The item id is `"daemon-health"`
3. The item is NOT currently active (same suppression as `showUnread`)

When daemon-health is the active view, the user is already looking at the health dashboard, so the amber dot is redundant.

**No new external dependencies**: Pure CSS + inline SVG + existing props patterns.

## Conventions Established

- **NavRail item id**: `"daemon-health"` — matches the ViewMode literal from sibling task `.7`.
- **NavRail label**: `"System Health"` — matches the breadcrumb label from sibling task `.7`.
- **Prop name**: `degraded?: boolean` — specific to daemon health status, not a generic badge.
- **CSS data-severity pattern**: `data-severity="degraded"` on `unreadIndicator` — uses `var(--color-warning)` for amber. The value `"degraded"` is distinct from `"warning"` to match the daemon health status vocabulary (`"ok" | "degraded" | "unhealthy"`), but uses the same amber color.
- **BOTTOM_ITEMS order**: daemon-health first, then settings. System status above configuration.

