ConcurrencyTracker methods Acquire, TryAcquire, Release, and Close all have nil receiver guards (if ct == nil { return ... }), but ActiveCount and Counts do not. This means calling these methods on a nil *ConcurrencyTracker will panic with a nil pointer dereference. Since the daemon can run without concurrency tracking configured (ct could be nil), these methods need the same nil guards for consistency and safety.

## Technical Approach

Add nil receiver checks at the top of both methods, matching the established pattern in the file. ActiveCount returns 0 on nil (no active counts). Counts returns an empty map on nil.

## Files to Modify

